tion):\n\n```markdown\n# Validate the GPU usage metering data contract for chargeback reporting\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the strategy's open question — the GPU usage metering data schema is owned by the in-flight metering strategy (RHAISTRAT-9990) and is not final until that strategy lands — by pinning down the consumption contract that GPU chargeback reporting builds against, before implementation effort is committed. It produces a documented, validated contract and a consumption decision that gate the reporting implementation work.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Document the metering data contract as currently defined by the in-flight GPU usage metering work: the data schema (fields, per-project attribution keys, time semantics), the access mechanism by which reporting will read the data, and the definition of a billable GPU-hour as delivered by the metering output.\n- Classify the contract's stability and finalization path with the metering strategy's owners: what is settled, what is still in flux, and the expected timeline.\n- Decide and record the consumption approach: build the reporting layer directly against the contract, or through an isolation layer that maps the metering output to the report data model so later schema changes stay contained.\n- Validate the reporting requirements (per-project GPU-hour reports over selectable time ranges, CSV export via a stable API, current-month consumption against Kueue quota) against the contract and record any deltas or constraints they impose.\n- Record the Kueue quota data source (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue as exposed by the platform's queueing stack) that the quota comparison will read, and confirm it is available independently of the metering work.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- The metering data contract (schema, access mechanism, GPU-hour semantics, per-project attribution) is documented, with each element classified as settled or in flux and the finalization path recorded.\n- A consumption decision is recorded — direct consumption vs. isolation layer — with rationale and a fallback for the case where the contract changes after reporting work starts.\n- Each reporting requirement is checked against the contract and the outcome (feasible as stated / needs adjustment) is recorded, so downstream scope is confirmed or rewritten.\n- The external timing risk of the metering strategy's landing is assessed and recorded as a risk note with its implications for the reporting schedule.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): the investigation validates that per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges can be computed from the metering data, and determines the approach.\n- HLR-2 (P1): the investigation confirms the data basis for CSV export and the stable API.\n- HLR-3 (P1): the investigation confirms the Kueue quota data source for the quota comparison.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The contract's finalization is externally controlled by the metering strategy's schedule; this investigation resolves what can be known now and structures the residual risk, it cannot force finality.\n```\n\nHmm — \"gate the reporting implementation work\" — fine, no sibling IDs.\n\n**E002 body** (Implementation):\n\n```markdown\n# Per-project GPU-hour report data service with CSV export and stable API\n\n## Description\n\nThis epic delivers the server-side GPU chargeback reporting capability of the Dashboard: computation of per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges from the metering data delivered by the GPU usage metering work, exposed through a stable API for the administrator-facing view and for finance tooling, with CSV export. It consumes the metering data as validated by the metering data contract investigation; it must not re-implement usage collection.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Compute per-project GPU-hour consumption aggregates over caller-selectable time ranges from the metering data (no re-implementation of the collection pipeline).\n- Expose the rep