{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e3e4383ccf1377f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9018\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9018: GPU chargeback reporting for platform administrators\n11\t\n12\t## Decomposition Notes\n13\t\n14\t**Staff Engineer Input**: none present in the strategy — no overrides to log.\n15\t\n16\t**Triage**: not below-threshold and not docs-only. The strategy spans two affected\n17\tcomponents (Dashboard, Distributed Workloads), multiple architecturally distinct\n18\twork streams (server-side reporting, web UI, substantive documentation authoring\n19\tper HLR-4), and its P0 requirement depends on an externally-owned data contract\n20\tthat the strategy explicitly flags as not final. Full decomposition follows.\n21\t\n22\t**Strategy structure health**: the strategy provides HLRs, an out-of-scope list,\n23\tand one open question, but is missing several sections the decomposition normally\n24\tconsumes: no Affected Components change/owner table (components are listed as\n25\tnames only), no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table (the RHAISTRAT-9990\n26\tdependency appears only in frontmatter links and the problem statement), no\n27\tGiven/When/Then acceptance criteria, no non-functional requirements, and no size\n28\tor effort estimate. Owner teams below are derived from the architecture context\n29\tfiles (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md` →\n30\tRHOAI Dashboard; `distributed-workloads.md` → Distributed Workloads).\n31\t\n32\t**Component mapping**:\n33\t\n34\t- All reporting epics use the canonical component `Dashboard` (verbatim from\n35\t  `.context/rhai-components.txt`).\n36\t- `Distributed Workloads` is a **passive** component in this strategy: no code\n37\t  change to it is described — it is the source of the Kueue quota\n38\t  (ClusterQueue/LocalQueue) figures that HLR-3 compares against consumption. Its\n39\t  validation is folded in as acceptance criteria on the reporting epics\n40\t  (reconciliation against the Kueue quota model), performed by the consuming\n41\t  Dashboard team; no separate validation epic is generated because no other team\n42\t  performs validation work on it.\n43\t- The docs epic uses the canonical component `Documentation`. The strategy names\n44\t  no owning team for it; it is assigned to the team that owns the reporting work,\n45\t  with the assumption flagged in Health Warnings.\n46\t\n47\t## Epic List\n48\t\n49\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n50\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 | Validate the GPU usage metering data contract for chargeback reporting | Investigation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 | Per-project GPU-hour report data service with CSV export and stable API | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n53\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Dashboard admin view for GPU chargeback reports and Kueue quota comparison | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P0 |\n54\t| RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | Document GPU chargeback report semantics and export integration | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n55\t\n56\tRHAISTRAT-9018-E004 carries `implementation_type: docs-authoring` (Red Hat docs\n57\tcontent process). Its priority derives from the strategy frontmatter\n58\t(`priority: Major` → P1) per the docs-authoring priority exception, not from\n59\tinheritance.\n60\t\n61\t## Dependency DAG\n62\t\n63\t```mermaid\n64\tgraph TD\n65\t    E001[\"RHAISTRAT-9018-E001 (Investigation)\"] --> E002[\"RHAISTRAT-9018-E002\"]\n66\t    E001 --> E003[\"RHAISTRAT-9018-E003\"]\n67\t    E002 --> E003\n68\t    E002 --> E004[\"RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 (docs-authoring)\"]\n69\t    E003 --> E004\n70\t```\n71\t\n72\t## DAG Justification\n73\t\n74\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n75\t|------|------|-----------|\n76\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementations) | The metering data contract (schema, access mechanism, GPU-hour semantics, stability) determines the data-access approach and report data model of the reporting service; the strategy explicitly flags the schema as not final. True gate — action `rewrite`: the service's consumption layer is rewritten if the contract differs from the assumption. |\n77\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream Implementations) | The same contract determines which figures and semantics the admin view can display; direct edge alongside the transitive path through the reporting service. True gate — action `rewrite`. |\n78\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (API producer → consuming Implementation) | The admin view's code builds against the report API and data model produced by the reporting data service. The two are separate architectural sub-systems within the Dashboard (server-side reporting vs. web UI), split per Rule 2's sub-system boundary axis. |\n79\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | The documentation describes the report semantics and export integration that this epic implements. |\n80\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by all Implementations) | The documentation describes the admin view this epic implements. |\n81\t\n82\tNo `repo-onboarding`, `konflux-onboarding`, or `license-validation` epics: both\n83\taffected components are in the platform inventory (architecture context files\n84\texist) and the Dashboard is already Konflux-onboarded per its context file, so\n85\tthe Rule 20 onboarding chain does not apply.\n86\t\n87\t**No branch epics**: the investigation's outcomes are bounded (contract stable\n88\tenough to consume directly vs. contract in flux requiring an isolation layer\n89\tbetween the metering output and the report data model), but both outcomes are\n90\tcovered by the same downstream epic set — they change the internal approach of\n91\tthe reporting service, not which epics exist or their boundaries. The gate is\n92\ttherefore recorded as `gated_by` + `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` on the\n93\tdownstream epics rather than as conditional branch files.\n94\t\n95\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n96\t\n97\t| HLR | Priority | Requirement | Epic | Notes |\n98\t|-----|----------|-------------|------|-------|\n99\t| HLR-1 | P0 | Administrators can view per-project GPU-hour consumption reports over selectable time ranges, computed from metering data | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002, RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 | Split by architectural sub-system (Rule 2): E002 computes and serves the reports (server side), E003 renders them in the admin view (web UI). Each half is necessary for the HLR. |\n100\t| HLR-2 | P1 | Reports exportable as CSV and retrieved via a stable API for finance tooling | RHAISTRAT-9018-E002 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: the CSV export and stable API serialize the same per-project GPU-hour aggregates over the same report data model the P0 reporting service computes — they share the data structure and API endpoint surface, so a split would double-touch the same files and build the same surface twice; not separately schedulable. |\n101\t| HLR-3 | P1 | Dashboard admin view shows current-month consumption against each project's Kueue quota | RHAISTRAT-9018-E003 (bundled) | Bundled with the P0 work: the quota comparison renders in the same admin report view and against the same report data as the P0 consumption report (HLR-1) — it shares the UI surface, so a split would build the same view twice; not separately schedulable. The quota figures themselves are served by the reporting data service as supporting data. |\n102\t| HLR-4 | P2 | Documentation covers report semantics (what counts as a GPU-hour) and export integration | RHAISTRAT-9018-E004 | docs-authoring epic, blocked by all Implementation epics per Rule 11. |\n103\t\n104\tAll four HLRs are covered; none orphaned.\n105\t\n106\t## Health Warnings\n107\t\n108\t- **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Impacted Teams table, no\n109\t  Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no NFRs, no\n110\t  size/effort estimate. Owner teams were derived from architecture context, and\n111\t  epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs and problem statement.\n112\t  Worth confirming team ownership — in particular the docs epic's owner — with\n113\t  the strategy author.\n114\t- **External timing risk** (the strategy's open question): the metering data\n115\t  schema finalizes only when RHAISTRAT-9990 lands. That timing is externally\n116\t  controlled and is therefore recorded here and in the epic risk notes, never as\n117\t  an acceptance criterion. It is mitigated structurally: the contract-validation\n118\t  investigation runs first and downstream epics carry a `rewrite` gate with a\n119\t  fallback (isolation layer over the metering output).\n120\t- **Epic size**: the strategy provides no effort estimate; all epics are scoped\n121\t  within the epic-size guideline (one investigation, one server-side service,\n122\t  one admin view, one documentation topic set — each a single sub-system and\n123\t  work-product type).\n124\t- **Priority consistency**: no inversions detected. Both reporting epics carry\n125\t  HLR-1 (P0); the investigation blocks all P0 work so it is P0 by inheritance;\n126\t  the docs epic takes P1 from the strategy's `Major` priority per the\n127\t  docs-authoring exception.\n128\t\n129\t## Tiered Delivery\n130\t\n131\tThe external dependency (the metering data delivered by RHAISTRAT-9990, with\n132\tuncertain timing) gates all user-facing value: no administrator-visible report\n133\tcan ship without the metering data, so there is no user-facing Tier 1.\n134\t\n135\t- **Tier 1 (starts immediately — risk-reduction)**: the contract-validation\n136\t  investigation. It reduces the external-timing risk by pinning down the\n137\t  consumption contract, its stability classification, and the\n138\t  direct-vs-isolation-layer decision before implementation effort is committed.\n139\t- **Tier 2 (gated)**: the reporting data service, the admin view, and the\n140\t  documentation — all blocked directly or transitively on the contract\n141\t  resolution. Per Rule 21, the in-flight metering strategy is modeled as a\n142\t  precondition with tiered delivery, not as an epic in this decomposition (the\n143\t  metering team resolves it; this strategy must not re-implement collection).\n144\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b92ef38b605fc8c9","agent_id":"a5bb3e126258997a9","session_id":"f1063ad1-f88e-4f24-95c5-f48c7743c72b","uuid":"1b03c56f-8e7c-4f8a-a08a-4cf059591b9a","timestamp":"2026-10-26T10:25:06.518Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e3e4383ccf1377f","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9018 decomposition"}
